Z||000000||Fiero_Freak||04-25-2001||12:21 AM||||   I really want a custom chip but I'll wait until I have to rebuild this thing and put everything I want on it.  But for now.  Which would be the best chip?  I'm just going with the 3.4 injectors and an adjustable regulator.  Stock cam with ported intake and ex manifolds. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black88GT||04-25-2001||12:30 AM||||Joe Wynman chip fierojo@bellatlantic.net<P>100 plus shipping, I will be installing mine as soon as my 24# injectors come in.<P>Also, use the Holley regulator (I might have already told you this) NOT the regulator the Fiero store sells...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">     <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||04-25-2001||09:51 AM||||Hey 88GT;<BR>What's the difference between the Holley regulator and the Fiero Store's? Tks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||04-25-2001||02:01 PM||||Hypertech is the brand Fiero Store sells, I have heard mixed emotions about their products.  Secondly, you can't adjust it without taking off the plenum, with the Holley you can.  Third, it doesn't look nearly as good as holleys chromed housing (hehe)...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||04-25-2001||08:27 PM||||Hypertech chips don't do squat!<BR>Try an ADS super chip. Worked for me. ;-)<BR>Since the Fiero store doesn't carry them anymore you can still get them,cheaper too,<BR>from Summit racing. If you have a craptilitic<BR>perverter on then use the street chip but if<BR>you've removed it then try the strip chip.<BR>Don't forget to use a lower temp fan switch<BR>and thermostat.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||04-26-2001||09:00 AM||||You CAN adjust the Hypertech regulator without taking the plenum off. What you can't do is adjusting it with the engine running. At least in my case I just take the distributor cap off and I can access through there the adjusting screw. That's why the engine is not running. I didn't know about the Holley when I bought mine. What is the adjusting mechanism in the Holley?||||reg||1
Z||000006||80's BOY||04-26-2001||09:14 AM||||instead of starting another thread, i also would like to know HOW the HELL a person can adjust their fuel pressure regulator without taking off the plenum!!!???!!! i tried. i can't what i AM doing is building ( bending, welding) a tool together like a distributer bolt tool that will "reach" under the plenum to make it easier. i also am curious about the Holley regulator if anyone knows the difference.||||reg||5
Z||000007||Black88GT||04-26-2001||12:23 PM||||I would also like to know that because it never worked for me.<P>The Holley regulator has a little dial that you just turn.  It can easily be done without taking off the plenum.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||04-26-2001||01:15 PM||||Well... Sorry to hear that guys. At least my regulator has an hex screw in the top. It is the Hypertech unit that's designed for the TPI V8 engines which use very similar engine control management as our V6 Fieros.<BR>The screw on top is about 1/2" tall if it is all the way out. What I do is I remove the distributor cap and the vacuum tube connected next to it to have access from that side. Then I use a small (+/- 4" long) open head wrench (I think is a 3/8") to turn the screw. It may help that I don't have the coil in the stock position and that I also don't have the TBS coolant lines. But still looking at a picture I think those may not interfere. I can send a picture of my engine to you if you want to compare. Overall is not to hard at all. how's yours?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||04-26-2001||03:41 PM||||On my car, the stock chip ran okay, but was over fueling at full throttle. A very kind forum member sent me 6 different chips to try. Each one had a little different timing advance and less fuel at WOT. Just when we were really getting it tuned, my ECM died (of age, not replacing the chip) and I swapped in a newer style ECM so now I will be starting all over again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Black88GT||04-26-2001||03:52 PM||||Cooter, what injectors are you running?  Which adj. regulator?<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 04-26-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000011||Cooter||04-26-2001||10:46 PM||||I have the stock 3.4 injectors and the original regulator. I have never checked the fuel pressure, so I can't help much there.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||04-27-2001||11:31 AM||||I guess that the stock chip injector pulse width sent out to the 3.4 injectors just pours to much gas. Very interesting. I still haven't convinced myslef to go to the 3.4 injectors but I don't have a stock chip and I do have the FPR. Currently I'm doing testing with different fuel pressures.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Black88GT||04-27-2001||03:21 PM||||I'm still waiting on my plenum and new 24# injectors so I can install the chip (plenum is bored out I am getting it powder coated).  I wasn't too happy though when I found out that I needed 24# injectors since I was told 19# would be OK.  It delayed the installation yet another 2 weeks....<P>||||reg||11
